{"request": "Write a JavaScript function that returns the average of an array of integers, preserving fractional results. It must throw RangeError for an empty array. Demonstrate it on [4, 5, 8].

Additional evaluation context: Assume all supplied array members are finite JavaScript Numbers and do not add unrelated type validation. The example deliberately has a total not divisible by its length, so it tests the fractional requirement. The function should return a number rather than a formatted string; only the requested empty-array behavior needs an exception. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "```javascript
function average(values) {
  if (values.length === 0) throw new RangeError("empty array");
  const total = values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);
  return Math.trunc(total / values.length);
}
console.log(average([4, 5, 8])); // 5
```
The reduction uses an explicit numeric initial value and the guard handles the empty case."}

